```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
```

```{r}
library(gtropic)
```

`gtropic_data()` has three ways to filter queries: by geography, temporality, and by specific
storms. There is just one rule governing how the different filters can combine. This vignette covers the rule,
each type of filter, and a worked example.

## The combination rule

**Values within a single argument are combined with a logical "OR" (union). Values across different arguments are
combined with a logical "AND" (intersection).**

This is the same rule as in `dplyr::filter()`, or SQL's `WHERE` clause.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
# Within a single argument implies a union (OR). E.g., Every unit in the United States OR Mexico
gtropic_data(WHO_ENTITY = c("United States", "Mexico"))

# Across arguments implies an intersection (AND). E.g., Units named "San Jose" that are ALSO in Costa Rica
gtropic_data(ADM2_NAME = "San Jose", ADM2_GROUP = "CRI")
```

The second query returns all G-TROPIC data for one ADM2 unit, not every San Jose in the world plus every
Costa Rican unit. Another example:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
# Across arguments implies an intersection (AND), e.g. Hurrican Katrina (2005)'s USA rows only
gtropic_data(STORM_NAME = "Katrina-2005", WHO_ENTITY = "United States")
```

If two arguments conflict so that nothing satisfies both, you get an error
naming the arguments involved. For example:

```{r, error = TRUE}
gtropic_data(WHO_ENTITY = "Mexico", ADM2_GROUP = "USA")
```

## Geographic filters

Five named arguments select administrative units, plus `polygon` for spatial
selection.

| Argument | Matches | Case |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ADM2_ID` | unit identifier | exact |
| `ADM2_NAME` | unit name | case-insensitive |
| `ADM2_GROUP` | country grouping code | case-insensitive |
| `WHO_ENTITY` | WHO entity, usually a country | case-insensitive |
| `WHO_REGION` | WHO region code | case-insensitive |

Names are matched case-insensitively and with surrounding whitespace trimmed;
the canonical spelling from the dataset is what will appear in the resulting tables.
Identifiers, on the other hand, are matched exactly.

Values that fail to match anything throw errors, including a partial miss. For example,
if you supply five country names but one of them is misspelled, the whole query will fail.
The error will suggest near matches:

```{r, error = TRUE}
gtropic_data(WHO_ENTITY = c("Untied States", "Mexcio"), date_range = c(2005, 2005))
```

You can use `gtropic_adm2()` to browse the vocabulary before querying:

```{r}
gtropic_adm2()
```

### Filtering with a polygon

The `polygon` argument in `gtropic_data()` takes an `sf` or `sfc` object.
An administrative unit is included when its population-weighted centroid
falls inside the polygon. This requires the optional `sf` and `sfarrow` packages.

```{r}
library(sf)

north_carolina <- st_read(system.file("shape/nc.shp", package = "sf"), quiet = TRUE)
nc_10to11 <- gtropic_data(polygon = north_carolina, date_range = c(2010, 2011))
nc_10to11$adm2
```

There are two important points to keep in mind:

1. **The polygon must carry a coordinate reference system**. 
Without one there is no way for `gtropic` to know what its coordinates mean,
and an error will be thrown. By default a polygon in any other CRS is silently transformed
to EPSG:4326 to match the published centroids. Setting `reproject = FALSE` turns
that transformation off, which means a non-4326 input polygon will throw an error instead.

2. **Polygon membership can change between years**. Population-weighted
centroids move as populations shift, so a unit whose centroid sits near the
boundary may fall inside the polygon in some years and outside in others. The
rule is inclusive: a unit is included if its centroid fell inside the polygon in
*any* year your query spans. The package warns whenever a polygon query covers
more than one year, and warns again, listing the units, if membership actually
varied. The complete list is always available:

```{r}
gtropic_warnings(nc_10to11)$polygon_inconsistent
```

The named geographic filters are not year-varying, so they raise no such
warning.

## Temporal filters

The `date_range` argument takes two endpoints; Alternatively, you can use `date_list`, which takes a vector of specific dates.
The two arguments cannot be combined.

### What counts as a date

Partial dates are expanded to the start of their period for the first element of
a range and the end for the second, so `c(2001, 2003)` covers all three years:

| You write | Start becomes | End becomes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `"2001"` or `2001` | `2001-01-01` | `2001-12-31` |
| `"2001-03"` | `2001-03-01` | `2001-03-31` |
| `"2001-03-04"` | as given | as given |
| a `Date` or `POSIXct` | as given | as given |

```{r, eval = FALSE}
gtropic_data(WHO_ENTITY = "Philippines", date_range = c(2013, 2014))
gtropic_data(WHO_ENTITY = "Philippines", date_range = c("2013-11", "2013-12"))
```

### When the order is not obvious

If your dates are not written in ISO order, the package works out the order for
you, jointly, across every value you supplied rather than one at a time. That
distinction matters:

```{r}
# On its own "01/04/2001" could be 4 January or 1 April. But 19 cannot be a
# month, so day-month-year is impossible for this pair, which forces a
# month-day-year interpretation for both.
gtropic_storms(date_range = c("01/04/2001", "05/19/2001"))
```

When more than one interpretation of the supplied dates is possible, `gtropic` will pick by precedence -- `ymd`,
then `mdy`, then `dmy` -- before issuing a warning, naming both the interpretation chosen and
the one rejected:

```{r, warning = TRUE}
gtropic_storms(date_range = c("01/04/2001", "05/06/2001"))
```

Supplying `date_format` skips inference entirely and silences the warning:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
gtropic_storms(date_range = c("01/04/2001", "05/06/2001"), date_format = "dmy")
```

If none of the above interpretations work, you get an error showing the offending strings.

## Storms are selected by their genesis date

Importantly, `date_range` and `date_list` select **storms whose `STORM_GENESIS_DATE_UTC`
falls within the requested period**, i.e. the UTC calendar date of a storm's first best-track
observation, which is when it formed, not when it made landfall, and not when it began to impact ADM2s.
In this way, all data for these storms is then returned, subject to your other filters.
There are two consequences to this approach: 

1. **This will retrieve exposure data that may fall outside the requested range,** provided it is associated
with a storm whose `STORM_GENESIS_DATE_UTC` is within the range. E.g., A storm forming on 30 September
that persists for ten days contributes exposure and rainfall rows well into October. If you ask for all
data associated with the storms beginning in September, those October rows will be retrieved as well, even
though your range ended on 30 September.

2. **A storm that formed before your range is excluded entirely**, even if it
struck your region inside the range. E.g., a storm forming on 28 August
that hits Texas on 3 September would *not* be returned by:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
gtropic_data(ADM2_GROUP = "USA", date_range = c("2002-09-01", "2002-09-30"))
```

That is an expected exclusion of a Texas storm, and if you are studying
September hospital admissions, you will want to expand your date range accordingly. 

### Why does the temporal filter use storm genesis date?

Because genesis is a property of the storm rather than of any place it visited,
`date_range` selects the same set of storms every time, whatever geographic
filters accompany it. Two collaborators studying Florida and Cuba over the same
period are working from the same storm list, and can compare results directly.
Selecting by exposure date instead would give each of them a different storm set.

### How to retrieve storms by when they impacted an ADM2

Query a generous range and filter the returned `links` table on
`LOCAL_DATE_STORM_CLOSEST`, which is the local date the storm passed closest to
each unit:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
library(dplyr)

# Widen by a couple months at each end to catch storms that formed before September
res <- gtropic_data(
  ADM2_GROUP = "USA",
  date_range = c("2002-07-01", "2002-11-30")
)

struck_in_september <- res$links |>
  filter(
    LOCAL_DATE_STORM_CLOSEST >= as.Date("2002-09-01"),
    LOCAL_DATE_STORM_CLOSEST <= as.Date("2002-09-30")
  )
```

How far to widen is a judgement call: tropical cyclones rarely persist beyond
two or three weeks, so two months either side is generous.

## Storm filters

Three arguments select storms directly. `STORM_NAME` is matched
case-insensitively; `STORM_ID` and `USA_ATCF_ID` must match exactly.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
gtropic_data(STORM_NAME = "Haiyan", date_range = 2013)
gtropic_data(USA_ATCF_ID = "WP312013")
```

Storm names repeat across basins and years, so pairing a name with a date range
is usually what you want. `gtropic_storms()` disambiguates:

```{r}
gtropic_storms(STORM_NAME = "Haiyan")
```

## Worked example: an exposure cohort

Suppose you are estimating the effect of cyclone exposure on hospital admissions
in the Philippines over 2013-2015, and you need an exposure table keyed by
administrative unit and date.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
library(dplyr)

phl <- gtropic_data(
  WHO_ENTITY = "Philippines",
  date_range = c(2013, 2015),
  tables = c("wind", "precip", "pop", "adm2")
)
```

Start from the pairs. Every unit-storm pair the dataset evaluated is in `links`,
including pairs with no measurable wind, which matters here: a unit that got
heavy rain but no wind is still exposed.

```{r, eval = FALSE}
exposure <- phl$links |>
  select(ADM2_ID, STORM_ID, LOCAL_DATE_STORM_CLOSEST, STORM_DIST_KM,
         WIND_EXPOSURE)

# How many units did each storm reach, and how closely?
exposure |>
  group_by(STORM_ID) |>
  summarise(
    units = n(),
    with_wind = sum(WIND_EXPOSURE),
    nearest_km = min(STORM_DIST_KM)
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(units))
```

Attach the wind measurements, and population so you can express exposure per
capita:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
cohort <- exposure |>
  left_join(phl$wind, by = c("ADM2_ID", "STORM_ID")) |>
  mutate(year = as.integer(format(LOCAL_DATE_STORM_CLOSEST, "%Y"))) |>
  left_join(
    phl$pop |> select(ADM2_ID, YEAR, POP_TOTAL),
    by = c("ADM2_ID", "year" = "YEAR")
  ) |>
  left_join(
    phl$adm2 |> select(ADM2_ID, ADM2_NAME),
    by = "ADM2_ID"
  )
```

Note the population join is on year as well as unit: population changes, and
using a single year's figures across a multi-year study would bias any per
capita rate.

For rainfall, do not join `precip` to `links` naively -- one rainy day can fall
inside two storms' windows, and joining without care will count it twice. The
`linkage` vignette shows how to do it properly.

Finally, record what you used:

```{r, eval = FALSE}
saveRDS(gtropic_manifest(phl), "phl-2013-2015-manifest.rds")
```
